::: {.doc-subject}

Format values as URL links.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/posit-dev/great-tables/blob/main/great_tables/_formats.py#L3494-L3684){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-Kind.FUNCTION}
```python
GT.fmt_url(
    columns=None,
    rows=None,
    label=None,
    as_button=False,
    color="auto",
    show_underline="auto",
    button_fill="auto",
    button_width=None,
    button_outline=None,
    target="_blank",
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

With `fmt_url()`, input URL strings in the table body are transformed into HTML anchor elements
(`<a>` tags). The URLs can be displayed as-is, with a static label, or with a label generated by
a function. The links can also be styled as buttons with customizable colors and dimensions.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[columns]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [SelectExpr]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The columns to target. Can either be a single column name or a series of column names
    provided in a list.

<code>[rows]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [RowSelectExpr]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   In conjunction with `columns=`, we can specify which of their rows should undergo
    formatting. The default is all rows, resulting in all rows in targeted columns being
    formatted. Alternatively, we can supply a row index, a list of row indices, or (for Polars
    DataFrames) a Polars expression such as `pl.col("x") > 0`.

<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | Callable[[str], str] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   An optional label to use for the link. If a string is provided, it will be used as the
    visible text for all links. If a callable is provided, it will be called with the URL string
    and should return the display label.

<code>[as_button]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Should the link be styled as a button? By default this is `False`.

<code>[color]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;auto&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The color of the link text. The default `"auto"` uses `"#008B8B"` (dark cyan) for regular
    links and `"#FFFFFF"` (white) for buttons. Any CSS color name or hex value can be used.

<code>[show_underline]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;auto&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Should the link be underlined? The default `"auto"` uses `True` for regular links and
    `False` for buttons. Set explicitly to `True` or `False` to override.

<code>[button_fill]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;auto&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The background color for button-style links. The default `"auto"` uses `"#4682B4"` (steel
    blue). Only used when `as_button=True`.

<code>[button_width]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The width of the button. Should be a CSS width string (e.g., `"150px"`). By default buttons
    size to their content.

<code>[button_outline]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The CSS outline for the button (e.g., `"2px solid #ccc"`). By default, a light gray outline
    is automatically added when the button fill color is very light, and hidden otherwise.

<code>[target]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;_blank&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The `target` attribute for the anchor element. Defaults to `"_blank"` to open links in a new
    tab. Set to `None` to open in the same tab.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [GT]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The GT object is returned. This is the same object that the method is called on so that we
    can facilitate method chaining.
:::

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Using a subset of the `towny` dataset, let's format the `website` column as URL links.

::: {#17d1f613 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import polars as pl
from great_tables import GT, md, data

towny_top = (
    data.pl.towny
    .filter(pl.col("csd_type") == "city")
    .select("name", "website", "population_2021")
    .sort("population_2021", descending=True)
    .head(10)
)

(
    GT(towny_top)
    .tab_header(
        title=md("The 10 Largest Municipalities in `towny`"),
        subtitle="Population values taken from the 2021 census.",
    )
    .fmt_integer(columns="population_2021")
    .fmt_url(columns="website")
    .cols_label(
        name="Name",
        website="Site",
        population_2021="Population",
    )
)
```

